I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply one is the text ```json\n{\"x\":1}\n{\"y\":2}\n```\n{\"z\":3}. Reply two is the text ```json\n{\"x\":1}\n{\"y\":4}\n```\n{\"z\":3}. In reply one the fence holds two objects, {\"x\":1} and {\"y\":2}; the last one inside the fence is {\"y\":2}, so the rule requires the check to return {\"y\":2}. In reply two the fence holds {\"x\":1} and {\"y\":4}; the last one inside the fence is {\"y\":4}, so the rule requires the check to return {\"y\":4}. The check, however, takes the whole fenced span as one candidate, which is not a single valid JSON object, so it skips the fenced pool and falls through to the top-level pool, whose last candidate is {\"z\":3} in both replies. Thus the check returns {\"z\":3} for both, giving the same answer where the rule requires different answers. The conjecture would be false if recover_json_object returned {\"y\":2} for reply one and {\"y\":4} for reply two, because then it would be telling the replies apart as the rule requires.